From: Xiaomi Mi MIX Linux port contributors
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm: don't unmap from the global VM on handle close

msm_gem_close() tears down the object's mappings in the closing
context's VM. GPUs without per-process address spaces (A5xx and older)
give every context the GPU's global VM, so closing a handle in one
process unmaps a buffer that another process still uses through dma-buf
(the same object in the same VM): a compositor sampling a client's
buffer, or a browser's GPU process sharing frames with its UI process.
That process's GPU work then reads and writes the stale address.

On the Mi MIX (Adreno 530) this shows as bursts of "gpu fault ...
TRANSLATION" on every screen rotation and while browsers render on the
GPU; each faulting address was inside a live buffer in one snapshot of
the GEM list and unmapped in the next. Preemption is not involved (same
faults with a single ring).

Leave mappings in the global VM on handle close, and tear them down in
msm_gem_vma_put() once no handle, dma-buf or framebuffer refers to the
object (vma_ref drops to zero), after the GPU is done with it. The VMAs
hold a reference to the object, so leaving them until the object is
freed (the first version of this change) leaked every buffer: 26,000
objects and 8 GB within an hour, the phone swapping itself to a crawl.

---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gem.c
@@ -51,6 +51,7 @@ static void put_iova_spaces(struct drm_gem_object *obj, struct drm_gpuvm *vm,
 
 static void msm_gem_close(struct drm_gem_object *obj, struct drm_file *file)
 {
+	struct msm_drm_private *priv = obj->dev->dev_private;
 	struct msm_context *ctx = file->driver_priv;
 	struct drm_exec exec;
 
@@ -73,6 +74,17 @@ static void msm_gem_close(struct drm_gem_object *obj, struct drm_file *file)
 	if (msm_context_is_vmbind(ctx))
 		return;
 
+	/*
+	 * Without per-process address spaces (e.g. A5xx) every context
+	 * uses the GPU's global VM. A buffer shared with another process
+	 * through dma-buf is the same object there, still mapped and used
+	 * by that process: tearing the mapping down here makes its GPU
+	 * work fault on the stale address. Leave it until the last handle
+	 * or dma-buf goes away (msm_gem_vma_put()).
+	 */
+	if (ctx->vm == priv->gpu->vm)
+		return;
+
 	/*
 	 * TODO we might need to kick this to a queue to avoid blocking
 	 * in CLOSE ioctl
@@ -101,6 +113,23 @@ void msm_gem_vma_put(struct drm_gem_object *obj)
 	if (atomic_dec_return(&to_msm_bo(obj)->vma_ref))
 		return;
 
+	/*
+	 * No handle, dma-buf or framebuffer refers to the object any more.
+	 * Contexts without per-process address spaces leave their mappings
+	 * in the GPU's global VM on handle close (see msm_gem_close()), and
+	 * those VMAs hold a reference to the object: tear them down now, or
+	 * the object is never freed.
+	 */
+	if (priv->gpu && priv->gpu->vm) {
+		struct drm_exec exec;
+
+		dma_resv_wait_timeout(obj->resv, DMA_RESV_USAGE_BOOKKEEP, false,
+				      MAX_SCHEDULE_TIMEOUT);
+		msm_gem_lock_vm_and_obj(&exec, obj, priv->gpu->vm);
+		put_iova_spaces(obj, priv->gpu->vm, true, "vma_put");
+		drm_exec_fini(&exec);     /* drop locks */
+	}
+
 	if (!priv->kms)
 		return;
 
